Q: Load a WPF BitmapImage from a System.Drawing.Bitmap A: <p>The accepted answer performs a chain of WPF -&gt; GDI HBitmap -&gt; GDI+ Bitmap conversion while leaking memory and a GDI handle, copying the bitmap twice, and losing alpha information (as HBitmaps don't support transparency). And the most upvoted answer performs a slow encoding+decoding session, converting every pixel format to 24 or 32 bpp ones (as per the GDI+ PNG encoder), losing the palette of indexed formats or the high-res color information of 48/64 bpp images.</p>
<p>If you don't mind using a 3rd party <a href="https://github.com/koszeggy/KGySoft.Drawing" rel="nofollow noreferrer">library</a> (disclaimer: written by me), just make sure you reference the <a href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/KGySoft.Drawing" rel="nofollow noreferrer">GDI+</a> and <a href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/KGySoft.Drawing.Wpf" rel="nofollow noreferrer">WPF</a>-specific packages, and then the conversion is literally just a couple of lines:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>// Gets an IReadableBitmapData of your original GDI+ bitmap.
// It does NOT copy it, just gets a fast pixelformat-specific accessor.
using var bitmapData = bitmap.GetReadReadableBitmapData();

// Practically a fast, optimized direct copy into a new a WriteableBitmap,
// using the closest compatible pixel format to the original bitmap.
return bitmapData.ToWriteableBitmap(); // which is a BitmapSource after all
</code></pre>
<p>Converting back is equally simple, just use the WPF-specific <a href="https://koszeggy.github.io/docs/drawing/html/M_KGySoft_Drawing_Wpf_BitmapSourceExtensions_GetReadableBitmapData_1.htm" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>GetReadableBitmapData</code></a> for any <code>BitmapSource</code>, and call the <a href="https://koszeggy.github.io/docs/drawing/html/M_KGySoft_Drawing_Imaging_ReadableBitmapDataExtensions_ToBitmap.htm" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>ToBitmap</code></a> extension to return a GDI+ bitmap.</p>

Q: I need an unordered list without any bullets A: <p>To completely remove the <code>ul</code> default style:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>list-style-type: none;

margin: 0;
margin-block-start: 0;
margin-block-end: 0;
margin-inline-start: 0;
margin-inline-end: 0;
padding-inline-start: 0;
</code></pre>

Q: Restricting the droppable vicinity of dynamically generated draggable div A: <p>accept does not restrict where the element can be dragged. It only controls whether a droppable accepts it.</p>
<p>Set the draggable to revert when it is not dropped on a valid host</p>
<pre><code>$('.MDTOptionsHostDiv').droppable({
  accept: '.liveBox',
  drop: function (event, ui) {
      ui.draggable.appendTo(this).css({
          top: 0,
          left: 0
      });
  }
});
</code></pre>
<p>Then initialize the new divs after the AJAX loop</p>
<pre><code>$hostDiv.find('.liveBox').draggable({
  revert: 'invalid'
});
</code></pre>
<p>The droppable only needs to be initialized once, not every time a new div is added.</p>
